 of \n\n$$\nG(x)=\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{2}x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n\\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-4,4).$ \nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{3}{2}t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ {[\\frac{3}{4}{t}^{2}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{12(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{4}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{4})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ \\frac{3}{4}{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{48(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}))\\end{aligned}\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317394 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "bff08d19-8cb4-5226-967f-942523bd104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "bff08d19-8cb4-5226-967f-942523bd104b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316201} :answer-field/id #uuid "bf1a59b6-d6c2-5e99-8bc5-ed1d49da21ea" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316995}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "086a93d6-9edf-5e6c-82a6-6ae6a865d444" :question/math-academy-id "q-343370" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\pi ,0] \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x+1, & x\\in (0,\\pi )\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{+}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $2\\pi $-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+1=2.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317396 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9e8d60e5-0ad9-55d5-8b7e-e475ef95acb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316010}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9e8d60e5-0ad9-55d5-8b7e-e475ef95acb2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316010} :answer-field/id #uuid "8b06dbb1-77e5-5ad6-bd29-3766a587c036" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316865}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "85b0a574-008a-56b0-af42-48f4058807a7" :question/math-academy-id "q-343385" :question/problem "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{2}, & x\\in [-\\frac{\\pi }{3},0] \\\\ 2\\operatorname{sin}⁡x, & x\\in (0,\\frac{\\pi }{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nConsider the function $F(x)$ shown above. Find the value to which the Fourier series of the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of $F(x)$ is converging when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$\n\nThe Fourier series converges to {{field-1}} when $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose that $f(x)$ is a $2L$-periodic piecewise smooth function with the corresponding Fourier series\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x)+{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi }{L}x).\n$$\n\nThen, the series converges for all $x\\in R.$\n\n- If $f(x)$ is continuous at $x,$ then the series converges to the value of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=f(x)\n$$\n\n- If $f(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x,$ then the series converges to the midpoint between the values of left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point:\n\n$$\nS(x)=\\frac{{f}^{-}(x)+{f}^{+}(x)}{2}\n$$\n\nwhere ${f}^{-}(x)$ and ${f}^{-}(x)$ are the left- and right-sided limits of the function at this point, respectively.\n\nNotice that the $\\frac{2\\pi }{3}$-periodic extension $f(x)$ of our function $F(x)$ is continuous at $x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.$ \n\nTherefore, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ converges to \n\n$$\nf\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=2sin\\,(\\frac{\\pi }{6})=1.\n$$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186317399 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "06015b37-c1da-5c8e-878c-60947fbc840c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315148} {:answer/id #uuid "24ea2114-3bd1-52c4-bf57-325363fd5bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315325} {:answer/id #uuid "a81de909-9548-5ec4-8d3c-1983bf705587" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{\\pi }" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316061}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "24ea2114-3bd1-52c4-bf57-325363fd5bb9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315325} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c781efb-4362-5ebb-94b7-8bf35aea1ea4" :answer-field/key "field-3" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045494 :db/ident :answer-field.type/select} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186316767} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5ed29729-d42b-5f9d-81b4-fac91b47193e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "discontinuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315651} {:answer/id #uuid "70cfcbc1-b8b9-5044-9c8f-66a69b6cebb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315754}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "70cfcbc1-b8b9-5044-9c8f-66a69b6cebb1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "continuous" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186315754} :answer-field/id #uuid "62a